 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery** (see `docs/decisions/change-delivery.md`). The server pushes committed event revisions to browsers over self-hosted WebSockets, paired with replayable revisions in the change store and client-side reconnect/catch-up logic. Chosen for low-latency updates without recurring realtime vendor fees or provider coupling, accepting the operational work of managing connection capacity and reconnect recovery. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected for higher read load and multi-second lag; a hosted realtime service was rejected due to recurring vendor costs and provider coupling.
